-# The DevTools Times
+# smallpt · Browser path tracing
 
-This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
+Kevin Beason's MIT-licensed [smallpt](https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/), compiled from C++ to WebAssembly with Emscripten. The Astro home page is prerendered to `dist/client/index.html`; it runs the tracer entirely in a browser Web Worker and paints progressive RGBA rows onto a canvas.
 
+## Build
 
 ```sh
-pnpm install && pnpm run start
+pnpm install
+pnpm run build
 ```
 
-The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
+The compiled module and its initial rendered preview are checked in under `public/smallpt`, so normal builds do not require Emscripten. To rebuild the C++ module with `emcc` installed:
 
-## Tech Stack
+```sh
+pnpm run build:wasm
+pnpm run build:preview
+pnpm run build
+```
+
+`build:preview` renders a 720 × 540, 128 spp preview using the same WASM module in Node. It does not start a server. `native/smallpt-original.cpp` is the unmodified upstream source; `native/smallpt-web.cpp` adapts its output and exposes color controls. The original mirror and glass materials, path integration, Cornell box, and 2×2 tent-filtered subpixel sampling are retained. Randomization changes the two foreground spheres' tint and starts a fresh render. Rendering quality, resolution, color picking, cancellation, reset, and PNG download are available on the page.
 
-- **[Astro](https://astro.build/)**: The web framework for building content-driven websites.
-- **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
-- **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
-- **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+The Emscripten worker does not use pthreads or require cross-origin isolation. Serve `.wasm` as `application/wasm` (Emscripten also supports an ArrayBuffer fallback). `native/LICENSE.txt` contains the upstream MIT license.
